That comprehension I wrote is convoluted and buggy. The problem: parser.items(section) includes DEFAULT keys. To exclude defaults that weren't overridden, we can't distinguish overridden vs inherited via items() alone... Actually configparser: if a section explicitly sets a key with the same value as default, items() returns it once — indistinguishable from inherited. A cleaner approach: read the file twice, or use a parser without defaults for section-only keys, plus a separate parse to get the DEFAULT dict.

Simplest: use two ConfigParser instances:
- p1 = normal; defaults = dict(p1.defaults()); sections with apply_defaults -> dict(p1.items(section))
- For non-apply-defaults: create a second parser where we strip the [DEFAULT] section before parsing (or set it after reading). E.g., read file into a StringIO, remove DEFAULT lines? Simpler: parse normally into p2, then p2._sections... private again.

Alternative clean approach without private APIs: read raw text, split off [DEFAULT] section manually, feed remainder into a fresh parser whose defaults are empty. That's straightforward.

